Compare the following two functionally equivalent Java implementations and determine which one runs faster.

Here are two example comparisons with explanations:

### Example 1:

Code A:
```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    int t = sc.nextInt();

    for (int i = 0; i < t; i++) {
      int n = sc.nextInt();
      int s = sc.nextInt();

      int result = s / (n / 2 + 1);
      System.out.println(result);
    }
  }
}

```

Code B:
```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

public class A {

  public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {

    BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));
    PrintWriter pw = new PrintWriter(new BufferedWriter(new OutputStreamWriter(System.out)));

    int t = Integer.parseInt(br.readLine());

    while (t-- > 0) {

      StringTokenizer st = new StringTokenizer(br.readLine());

      int n = Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken());
      int s = Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken());

      n++;

      if (n % 2 == 1) n++;

      n /= 2;

      System.out.println(s / n);
    }
  }
}

```

Both implementations reduce the result to the same integer quotient. Code A reads every number through Scanner.nextInt. Code B reads buffered lines and parses tokens directly. Its extra arithmetic and unused writer initialization do not remove its parsing advantage over many instances. I select Code B.

**Answer: B**

