import java.io.PrintWriter;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {

  void solve(Scanner in, PrintWriter out) {
    long n = in.nextLong();
    int res = 0;

    for (int i = 1; i <= Math.sqrt(n); ++i) {
      if (n % i == 0) {
        if (n / i == i) res++;
        else res += 2;
      }
    }
    System.out.println(res);
  }

  public static void main(String[] args) {
    try (Scanner in = new Scanner(System.in);
        PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(System.out)) {
      new Main().solve(in, out);
    }
  }
}

```

Code B:
```java
import java.math.BigInteger;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    BigInteger b = sc.nextBigInteger();
    BigInteger ans = BigInteger.valueOf(0);
    BigInteger one = BigInteger.valueOf(1);
    BigInteger a;
    for (a = BigInteger.valueOf(1);
        a.multiply(a).compareTo(b) == 0 || a.multiply(a).compareTo(b) < 0;
        a = a.add(one)) {
      if (b.mod(a).compareTo(BigInteger.ZERO) == 0) {
        if (a.compareTo(b.divide(a)) == 0) {
          ans = ans.add(BigInteger.valueOf(1));
        } else {
          ans = ans.add(BigInteger.valueOf(2));
        }
      }
    }
    System.out.println(ans);
  }
}

```

Both implementations enumerate possible divisors through the square root and count paired divisors. Code A uses primitive integer arithmetic. Code B repeatedly multiplies, divides, and takes remainders with BigInteger, introducing more arithmetic and object overhead. The stated input fits the primitive types used by Code A. I select Code A.

**Answer: A**


